The hardest concept for me so far has been understanding when different quantities are conserved, especially when angular momentum stays constant but kinetic energy increases. It takes a lot of breaking down individual equations and reading to get a real understanding of these concepts rather than just being able to regurgitate what is taught in the code lessons. This week, it clicked that conservation of angular momentum does not automatically mean kinetic energy is conserved. In the skater example, pulling the arms inward decreases the moment of inertia and increases angular speed, while the extra kinetic energy comes from work done by the person's muscles. Connecting rotation to familiar ideas like force, mass, and acceleration also helped me understand the rotational equations.
